<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  What the hardened runtime is asked to give back.

  Notarization requires the hardened runtime (codesign's `options
  runtime` flag), and the hardened runtime revokes a set of capabilities
  that an unsigned process has by default. Each entry below is one of
  those, turned back on. The list is deliberately short: every
  entitlement is a restriction lifted, and one added "just in case" is a
  hole with no test that would ever notice it.

  Note for anyone editing this file: an XML comment may not contain two
  consecutive hyphens, so command-line flags are spelled in prose here.
  Getting that wrong does not produce a comment with a typo in it, it
  produces a file codesign refuses to parse.

  Only one entitlement is needed, and it is the one whose absence is
  invisible:

  * com.apple.security.device.audio-input. Without it the capture stream
    opens, the system draws the microphone indicator in the menu bar,
    and every sample that arrives is zero. So receive runs, the
    waterfall stays black, and nothing anywhere says why. This is the
    same failure NSMicrophoneUsageDescription guards against in
    Info.plist, and both are required: the usage string is what the
    prompt says, this is what makes the process eligible to be asked.

  Three that are conspicuously absent, because each was considered and
  none is needed:

  * disable-library-validation. Under the hardened runtime a process may
    only load libraries signed by the same team (or by Apple). Every
    dylib in the bundle, meaning Qt, libhamlib, onnxruntime and the Qt
    plugins, is signed by tools/sign.sh with this project's identity, so
    validation passes. Adding it would let the app load anyone's code
    and would hide a genuine signing gap in the bundle.
  * allow-unsigned-executable-memory and allow-jit. onnxruntime's CPU
    execution provider interprets a graph; it does not generate code.
  * files.user-selected.read-write. That is a *sandbox* entitlement and
    means nothing outside the App Store sandbox, which this app is not
    in. The hardened runtime does not restrict file access at all.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
